            <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 overflow-hidden"><button type="button" class="faq-trigger w-full min-h-[52px] px-5 py-3.5 text-left flex justify-between items-center font-semibold" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="sc-f3"><span>Is scaling painful?</span><svg class="faq-chevron w-5 h-5 text-[#C1121F]" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M19.5 8.25l-7.5 7.5-7.5-7.5"/></svg></button><div id="sc-f3" class="hidden px-5 pb-4 text-slate-600">It is generally comfortable and requires no injections. Topical numbing gel can be used if your gums are extra sensitive.</div></div>
            <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 overflow-hidden"><button type="button" class="faq-trigger w-full min-h-[52px] px-5 py-3.5 text-left flex justify-between items-center font-semibold" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="sc-f4"><span>How often should I get scaling done?</span><svg class="faq-chevron w-5 h-5 text-[#C1121F]" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M19.5 8.25l-7.5 7.5-7.5-7.5"/></svg></button><div id="sc-f4" class="hidden px-5 pb-4 text-slate-600">We usually recommend a dental check-up and scaling every 6 to 12 months.</div></div>
